.\" ls(1): EmiBSD's own, written from scratch under its definition.
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt LS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ls
.Nd list directory contents
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm ls
.Op Fl 1AacdFghiklnpqRrSstuHL
.Op Fl o Ar format
.Op Fl -sort-by Ns = Ns Ar .field
.Op Fl -type Ns = Ns Ar kind , Ns Ar ...
.Op Fl -since Ns = Ns Ar duration
.Op Fl -max-depth Ns = Ns Ar n
.Op Fl -no-headers
.Op Fl -flags
.Op Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
lists files.
Each operand that is not a directory is listed as itself;
each directory operand is listed as its entries, and with
.Fl R
so is every directory below it.
With no operand the current directory is listed.
.Pp
Every file is a record with the fields of
.Pa schemas/entry.v1.json :
name, path, type, mode, owner, group, size, blocks, flags, inode,
the three times, the device numbers and a symbolic link's target.
The records are filtered, sorted and printed in the form
.Fl o
selects.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o Ar format , Fl -output Ar format
What to print:
.Cm name
(one name per line to a pipe, columns on a terminal),
.Cm table
(the long listing),
.Cm wide
(every field),
.Cm json ,
.Cm yaml ,
or
.Cm custom-columns= Ns Ar HEADER:.field , Ns Ar ...
.It Fl 1
One name per line.
The default when the output is not a terminal.
.It Fl C
Names in columns down the page.
The default on a terminal.
.It Fl l
The long table: mode, links, owner, group, size, time and name.
.It Fl g
The long table without the owner.
.Fl l
given anywhere overrides it.
.It Fl n
The long table with numeric owner and group.
.It Fl -sort-by Ns = Ns Ar .field
Sort by any field of the record, for example
.Ar .size ,
.Ar .mtime ,
.Ar .owner .
.It Fl t
Sort by time, newest first.
.It Fl S
Sort by size, largest first.
.It Fl r , Fl -reverse
Reverse the order.
.It Fl c
Use the time of last status change for sorting and in the table.
.It Fl u
Use the time of last access.
.It Fl a , Fl -all
Include entries whose names begin with a dot, and
.Sq \&.
and
.Sq \&.. .
.It Fl A , Fl -almost-all
Include dot entries except
.Sq \&.
and
.Sq \&.. .
Always on for the superuser.
.It Fl d , Fl -directory
List a directory itself, not its contents.
Turns
.Fl R
off.
.It Fl R , Fl -recursive
List subdirectories recursively.
.It Fl -max-depth Ns = Ns Ar n
With
.Fl R ,
descend at most
.Ar n
levels.
.It Fl -type Ns = Ns Ar kind , Ns Ar ...
Keep only entries of these kinds:
.Cm file , dir , symlink , fifo , socket , char , block .
.It Fl -since Ns = Ns Ar duration
Keep only entries modified within the last
.Ar duration :
.Cm 7d , 12h , 30m , 45s .
.It Fl F
Append
.Sq /
to directories,
.Sq *
to executables,
.Sq @
to symbolic links,
.Sq =
to sockets and
.Sq |
to FIFOs.
.It Fl p
Append
.Sq /
to directories.
.It Fl i
Print the inode number.
.It Fl s
Print the blocks used, in units of
.Ev BLOCKSIZE .
.It Fl k
Blocks of 1024 bytes for
.Fl s
and the total line.
.It Fl h , Fl -human
Sizes with K, M, G suffixes.
The default on a terminal.
.It Fl -flags
Show the file flags of
.Xr chflags 1
in the table.
.It Fl H
Follow symbolic links named on the command line.
The default unless
.Fl d , F
or
.Fl l
is given.
.It Fl L
Follow every symbolic link.
.It Fl q
Print
.Sq \&?
for non-printable characters.
The default on a terminal.
.It Fl -no-headers
No header line on tables.
.It Fl -help
Describe the program.
With
.Fl o Cm json
or
.Fl o Cm yaml
the description is a record,
.Pa lib/crates/cli/schemas/command-help.v1.json ,
for a tool or an agent that drives
.Nm .
.El
.Pp
Of a group of mutually exclusive options the last one given wins,
except that
.Fl l
always overrides
.Fl g .
.Ss Output to a pipe and to a terminal
When standard output is not a terminal the output of
.Fl l , 1 , i
and
.Fl s
is POSIX's, byte for byte what the standard's
.Nm
prints, so scripts that parse it keep working:
a
.Li total
line before each directory, then mode, links, owner, group, size
.Pq or Li major, minor ,
the date as
.Li Mon dd HH:MM
within six months and
.Li Mon dd  YYYY
beyond or in the future, and the name,
followed by
.Li \-\*(Gt target
for a symbolic link.
.Pp
On a terminal the defaults are different:
names in columns, a header line on tables, dates as
.Li YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM ,
human-readable sizes, and
.Sq \&?
in place of any character that could drive the terminal.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width BLOCKSIZE
.It Ev BLOCKSIZE
The unit of
.Fl s
and of the total line; 512 bytes unless set or
.Fl k .
.It Ev COLUMNS
The width used for columns; otherwise the terminal's, otherwise 80.
.It Ev TZ
The time zone dates are shown in.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 when every operand and entry was listed; 1 when an operand or an
entry could not be listed, or standard output failed, the rest having been
listed; and 2 when the command line was wrong, nothing having been listed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The classic long listing:
.Pp
.Dl $ ls -l
.Pp
Sizes without parsing columns:
.Pp
.Dl $ ls -o json | jq '.items[].size'
.Pp
What changed this week, oldest first:
.Pp
.Dl $ ls --sort-by=.mtime -r --since=7d
.Pp
Three fields of your choice:
.Pp
.Dl $ ls -o custom-columns=NAME:.name,OWNER:.owner,MODE:.perm
.Pp
The program's own description, as a record:
.Pp
.Dl $ ls --help -o json
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chflags 1 ,
.Xr chmod 1 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr symlink 7
.Sh STANDARDS
To a pipe,
.Nm
prints what
.St -p1003.1-2008
specifies for the flags it shares with it.
.Fl o
is the output selector of this system's common grammar and differs from the
X/Open
.Fl o .
.Fl -sort-by , -type , -since , -max-depth , -no-headers , -flags
and the terminal defaults are extensions.
.Sh HISTORY
This
.Nm
was written for EmiBSD in 2026, from a definition, with POSIX's
.Nm
as the reference for behaviour.
